1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
We already have AI inside Slack.

2
00:00:02,825 --> 00:00:06,414
You do not need another website or a new
workflow to get started.

3
00:00:07,062 --> 00:00:13,337
Click Chat with Slackbot AI in the top bar, or
use Control, Shift, O on Windows.

4
00:00:14,169 --> 00:00:16,184
This opens your private work assistant.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:21,367
It can use the Slack channels and documents you
already have permission to see,

6
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:25,225
and it cannot give you access to anything
outside those permissions.

7
00:00:26,086 --> 00:00:29,907
The goal is simple: use AI to find context,
organize work,

8
00:00:29,907 --> 00:00:33,256
and create a first draft without leaving Slack.

9
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:36,259
This is the fastest way to start the day.

10
00:00:36,924 --> 00:00:40,797
Ask which direct messages or mentions need your
attention first,

11
00:00:40,797 --> 00:00:44,004
and request the top five with links and next
actions.

12
00:00:44,692 --> 00:00:49,093
A specific format turns a general answer into
something you can use immediately.

13
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:51,920
If the first result is too broad,

14
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:57,545
follow up with "only include client work," or
"turn this into a checklist."

15
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:01,100
You do not need to rewrite the full prompt every
time.

16
00:01:01,751 --> 00:01:04,295
Keep the conversation focused and use short

17
00:01:04,295 --> 00:01:07,431
follow-ups until the output matches the way you
work.

18
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:11,463
When a channel moves quickly, name the channel,

19
00:01:11,463 --> 00:01:14,979
give a time window, and ask for a specific
output.

20
00:01:15,622 --> 00:01:20,522
Decisions, action items, owners, and open
questions is a useful default.

21
00:01:21,307 --> 00:01:26,387
You can also paste a link to one Slack thread
and ask for only the context in that thread.

22
00:01:27,210 --> 00:01:30,950
This works well before a meeting or when you
have been away for part of the day.

23
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:34,919
Always open the linked messages before you rely
on the summary.

24
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,500
The assistant saves reading time, but the source
messages remain the final record.

25
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:43,790
AI is best used as a first-draft partner.

26
00:01:44,479 --> 00:01:50,359
Give it the thread, tell it the structure you
want, and explicitly say not to send anything.

27
00:01:51,111 --> 00:01:57,272
Completed work, blockers, and next step is a
strong format for most client updates.

28
00:01:57,292 --> 00:02:02,692
Then review the names, dates, numbers,
commitments, and tone before the message leaves
Slack.

29
00:02:03,225 --> 00:02:08,865
If the draft feels generic, ask it to make the
wording warmer, shorter, or more direct.

30
00:02:09,683 --> 00:02:13,743
The account manager is still responsible for the
final client communication.

31
00:02:14,659 --> 00:02:17,419
Give the meeting length, the person, and the
sources.

32
00:02:18,162 --> 00:02:21,422
Then refine the result with short follow-ups
such as

33
00:02:21,922 --> 00:02:26,802
"make it shorter," "put the decision first," or
"turn this into a checklist."

34
00:02:27,376 --> 00:02:30,598
This is especially useful when you have ten
minutes before a

35
00:02:30,598 --> 00:02:34,201
call and need the key context without rereading
a week of Slack.

36
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:40,520
A good agenda should make the desired decision
clear, identify any unresolved questions,

37
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:44,300
and leave the last few minutes for owners and
next steps.

38
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:49,240
Slackbot is personal, but the work can still be
collaborative.

39
00:02:49,677 --> 00:02:51,026
When a prompt works well,

40
00:02:51,026 --> 00:02:55,405
share the exact prompt and the reviewed result
in the relevant channel.

41
00:02:56,087 --> 00:03:00,227
That gives the team a reusable starting point
instead of a one-time answer.

42
00:03:01,057 --> 00:03:04,657
Save the best prompts for recurring work such as
morning priorities,

43
00:03:05,397 --> 00:03:09,417
weekly client summaries, meeting preparation,
and quality checks.

44
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:13,810
Remember that each person's answers follow their
own Slack access,

45
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:18,910
so two people may get different context if their
channel permissions are different.

46
00:03:19,774 --> 00:03:23,040
Never paste passwords, API keys, MFA codes,

47
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:27,294
payment details, or sensitive client data into a
prompt.

48
00:03:27,922 --> 00:03:33,402
Verify the facts that matter, especially names,
numbers, dates, and commitments.

49
00:03:33,919 --> 00:03:36,819
AI can draft, summarize, and organize,

50
00:03:37,419 --> 00:03:39,782
but it should not send, publish, spend,

51
00:03:39,782 --> 00:03:43,539
or change an account without the responsible
human's approval.

52
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,443
If a response makes a claim that is not linked
to a source,

53
00:03:47,443 --> 00:03:51,760
ask the assistant to show where it came from or
mark it as needing verification.

54
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:59,300
The three habits are simple: give context, ask
for a format, and review before action.

55
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:03,372
Start small. Use one real prompt today,

56
00:04:03,912 --> 00:04:07,316
check the source, improve the result with one
follow-up,

57
00:04:07,316 --> 00:04:10,052
and save the version that works for the team.

58
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:13,014
One real prompt, one reviewed result,

59
00:04:13,014 --> 00:04:16,733
and one saved template is enough to turn AI from
a novelty

60
00:04:16,733 --> 00:04:20,261
into a repeatable part of the account manager
workflow.

